nodecode-zeromem is a rewrite of ptaranat's ptaranat/zeromem:

  https://github.com/ptaranat/zeromem
  commit f7e669c47cc0f6c9867829c37af8e4fc2803fe15

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Panat Taranat (LICENSE)

What was rewritten, and what was left out. The rewrite keeps the original's
memory path in Common Lisp: every user and assistant line of every session is
kept verbatim, with its session, turn, speaker and day, in one local JSONL
ledger under the home; a model asks for past words through the same three
tools (zeromem_recall, zeromem_stats, zeromem_forget_session); the turns of
past sessions that answer a turn's own input ride into its first model
request without anyone asking; forgetting a session drops it for good; and
/zeromem import is the operator's own read of a home that already has
sessions, as `hermes zeromem import` was. Ranking is the original's sparse
half -- BM25 over content tokens with its K1 and B, plus an exact-phrase
bonus, newest first on a tie -- and the same stopword list.

Left out: the Rust core's dense embedder (bge-small-en-v1.5 on ONNX, and the
130MB first-run download that came with it) and, with it, its two-view
retrieval: the entity graph, the hierarchical windows and episodes, and the
min-max fusion of the two views. What a query gets here is the lexical view
alone, which is why an exact name, a date or a quoted phrase is found by
this rewrite and a merely synonymous one may not be. Also left out: the
Hermes memory-provider wiring (prefetch threads, read-only contexts,
session switches), which Nodecode's own turn loop and hook points make
unnecessary; and `hermes sessions export`, since a Nodecode home's sessions
are already facts in its own event log, read in place.
